[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [[:db/retract 17592186122468 :answer-field/choices 17592186122469] [:db/retract 17592186122468 :answer-field/choices 17592186122470] [:db/retract 17592186122468 :answer-field/choices 17592186122471] [:db/retract 17592186122468 :answer-field/choices 17592186122472] [:db/retract 17592186122468 :answer-field/choices 17592186122473] [:db/retract 17592186122414 :answer-field/choices 17592186122416] [:db/retract 17592186122414 :answer-field/choices 17592186122419] {:db/id 17592186124464 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" "question-056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124978 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2x+4y+5xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(5y+2)\\text{ }i+(5x+4)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(5\\cdot 1+2)\\text{ }i+(5\\cdot 2+4)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =7i+14j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,1)}{‖∇f(2,1)‖} & =\\frac{7i+14j}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(14)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7i+14j}{7\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122468 :answer-field/correct "answer-cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c60959a-01b7-53b2-91be-83d1b906ea40" "answer-144e5910-b093-5cd4-abe0-394d07cded53" "answer-91e9dfc1-cddb-5022-b3ab-97f0a0267baa" "answer-cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98" "answer-812ac18a-9cbd-56eb-aadd-016577600a1e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c60959a-01b7-53b2-91be-83d1b906ea40" :answer/id #uuid "3c60959a-01b7-53b2-91be-83d1b906ea40" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,i+7\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-144e5910-b093-5cd4-abe0-394d07cded53" :answer/id #uuid "144e5910-b093-5cd4-abe0-394d07cded53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,i-12\\,j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91e9dfc1-cddb-5022-b3ab-97f0a0267baa" :answer/id #uuid "91e9dfc1-cddb-5022-b3ab-97f0a0267baa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i+j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98" :answer/id #uuid "cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-812ac18a-9cbd-56eb-aadd-016577600a1e" :answer/id #uuid "812ac18a-9cbd-56eb-aadd-016577600a1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "i-4j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124969 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4x+0-0)\\,i+(0+4y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =4x\\,i+(4y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =4(0)\\,i+(4(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∇f(0,1)}{‖∇f(0,1)‖}=\\frac{3\\,j}{3}=j\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186122414 :answer-field/correct 17592186122417 :answer-field/choices ["answer-f9c75248-f110-5d02-9855-a77023469031" "answer-1c93b689-6924-505a-914c-f48d03994cf6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9c75248-f110-5d02-9855-a77023469031" :answer/id #uuid "f9c75248-f110-5d02-9855-a77023469031" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-i-j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c93b689-6924-505a-914c-f48d03994cf6" :answer/id #uuid "1c93b689-6924-505a-914c-f48d03994cf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}j" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" :question/id #uuid "726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-37231" :question/problem "Find parametric equations for the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=1$ at the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}).$ Then,  our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$  \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}) & =\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{e}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe parametric equations for the normal line to $f(x,y,z)$ at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ are given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={x}_{0}+{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ y & ={y}_{0}+{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ z & ={z}_{0}+{f}_{z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametic equations  for the normal line to our surface that  passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be written as \n \n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0,\\,z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/id #uuid "c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-84d505d2-2d10-57ad-9f74-f1753c66adec" "answer-f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2" "answer-f43507f5-6454-586e-8980-28bd1f31c21d" "answer-ad368c2c-26d6-548c-b643-eedd79a18a15" "answer-7aae3ac3-02f2-5d2e-bd85-9eee8e2b917d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84d505d2-2d10-57ad-9f74-f1753c66adec" :answer/id #uuid "84d505d2-2d10-57ad-9f74-f1753c66adec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-t,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2" :answer/id #uuid "f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f43507f5-6454-586e-8980-28bd1f31c21d" :answer/id #uuid "f43507f5-6454-586e-8980-28bd1f31c21d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=0,\\text{ }y=t,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+2t\\sqrt{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad368c2c-26d6-548c-b643-eedd79a18a15" :answer/id #uuid "ad368c2c-26d6-548c-b643-eedd79a18a15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=t,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7aae3ac3-02f2-5d2e-bd85-9eee8e2b917d" :answer/id #uuid "7aae3ac3-02f2-5d2e-bd85-9eee8e2b917d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=t,\\text{ }y=t,\\text{ }z=\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" :question/id #uuid "056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" :question/math-academy-id "q-131125" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=4-4z$ at the point $(0,2,2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =4-4z \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)k \\\\  & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j+4\\,k \\\\ ∇f(0,2,2) & =2(0)\\,i-2(2)\\text{ }j+4\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,2,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,2,2)=⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/id #uuid "5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b3d1a48-5c55-52da-b7e7-fd31db815995" "answer-af75210d-d1a7-57de-a250-0369260017a5" "answer-77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05" "answer-63801628-2a57-5777-861f-90cd5a83b242" "answer-161e6fd0-e925-55fa-8d09-6a65d107f751"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b3d1a48-5c55-52da-b7e7-fd31db815995" :answer/id #uuid "3b3d1a48-5c55-52da-b7e7-fd31db815995" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-4,-4⟩+t\\,⟨0,2,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af75210d-d1a7-57de-a250-0369260017a5" :answer/id #uuid "af75210d-d1a7-57de-a250-0369260017a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,-4,-4⟩+t\\,⟨2,2,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05" :answer/id #uuid "77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63801628-2a57-5777-861f-90cd5a83b242" :answer/id #uuid "63801628-2a57-5777-861f-90cd5a83b242" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-161e6fd0-e925-55fa-8d09-6a65d107f751" :answer/id #uuid "161e6fd0-e925-55fa-8d09-6a65d107f751" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125735 :tutorial/content "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$ *\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental  link between\ncalculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)=  x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$  It is easy to see that  $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ \n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$ *\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$  is parallel to the level curve.\n\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot  \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125740 :tutorial/content "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if  the  gradient vector is nonzero, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$ \n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt]\n&= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt]\n\n\\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle.\n}\n$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124731 :activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}] "assertions" [[17592186124978 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2x+4y+5xy$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1).$"] [17592186124978 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(2,1)$, can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+4y+5xy)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(5y+2)\\text{ }i+(5x+4)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(5\\cdot 1+2)\\text{ }i+(5\\cdot 2+4)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =7i+14j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(2,1)}{‖∇f(2,1)‖} & =\\frac{7i+14j}{\\sqrt{{7}^{2}+(14)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7i+14j}{7\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124978 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-3c60959a-01b7-53b2-91be-83d1b906ea40" :answer/id #uuid "3c60959a-01b7-53b2-91be-83d1b906ea40"] ["answer-3c60959a-01b7-53b2-91be-83d1b906ea40" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3c60959a-01b7-53b2-91be-83d1b906ea40" :answer/value "7\\,i+7\\,j"] ["answer-144e5910-b093-5cd4-abe0-394d07cded53" :answer/id #uuid "144e5910-b093-5cd4-abe0-394d07cded53"] ["answer-144e5910-b093-5cd4-abe0-394d07cded53" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-144e5910-b093-5cd4-abe0-394d07cded53" :answer/value "7\\,i-12\\,j"] ["answer-91e9dfc1-cddb-5022-b3ab-97f0a0267baa" :answer/id #uuid "91e9dfc1-cddb-5022-b3ab-97f0a0267baa"] ["answer-91e9dfc1-cddb-5022-b3ab-97f0a0267baa" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-91e9dfc1-cddb-5022-b3ab-97f0a0267baa" :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(2\\,i+j)"] ["answer-cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98" :answer/id #uuid "cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98"] ["answer-cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98" :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(i+2\\,j)"] ["answer-812ac18a-9cbd-56eb-aadd-016577600a1e" :answer/id #uuid "812ac18a-9cbd-56eb-aadd-016577600a1e"] ["answer-812ac18a-9cbd-56eb-aadd-016577600a1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-812ac18a-9cbd-56eb-aadd-016577600a1e" :answer/value "i-4j"] [17592186122468 :answer-field/correct "answer-cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98"] [17592186122468 :answer-field/choices "answer-3c60959a-01b7-53b2-91be-83d1b906ea40"] [17592186122468 :answer-field/choices "answer-144e5910-b093-5cd4-abe0-394d07cded53"] [17592186122468 :answer-field/choices "answer-91e9dfc1-cddb-5022-b3ab-97f0a0267baa"] [17592186122468 :answer-field/choices "answer-cef8e14b-ece7-51a1-921e-23d70f9d2c98"] [17592186122468 :answer-field/choices "answer-812ac18a-9cbd-56eb-aadd-016577600a1e"] [17592186124969 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,1).$"] [17592186124969 :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(0,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4x+0-0)\\,i+(0+4y-1)\\,j \\\\  & =4x\\,i+(4y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,1) & =4(0)\\,i+(4(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∇f(0,1)}{‖∇f(0,1)‖}=\\frac{3\\,j}{3}=j\n$$"] [17592186124969 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-f9c75248-f110-5d02-9855-a77023469031" :answer/id #uuid "f9c75248-f110-5d02-9855-a77023469031"] ["answer-f9c75248-f110-5d02-9855-a77023469031" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f9c75248-f110-5d02-9855-a77023469031" :answer/value "-i-j"] ["answer-1c93b689-6924-505a-914c-f48d03994cf6" :answer/id #uuid "1c93b689-6924-505a-914c-f48d03994cf6"] ["answer-1c93b689-6924-505a-914c-f48d03994cf6" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1c93b689-6924-505a-914c-f48d03994cf6" :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}i+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}j"] [17592186122414 :answer-field/correct 17592186122417] [17592186122414 :answer-field/choices "answer-f9c75248-f110-5d02-9855-a77023469031"] [17592186122414 :answer-field/choices "answer-1c93b689-6924-505a-914c-f48d03994cf6"] ["question-726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" :question/id #uuid "726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44"] ["question-726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" :question/math-academy-id "q-37231"] ["question-726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" :question/problem "Find parametric equations for the normal line to the surface $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})=1$ at the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$"] ["question-726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" :question/worked-solution "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}).$ Then,  our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=1.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}).$  \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,0,\\sqrt{e}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))k \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,\\sqrt{e}) & =\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }i+\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }j+\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{(0)^{2}+(0)^{2}+(\\sqrt{e})^{2}}\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{e}}{e}k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe parametric equations for the normal line to $f(x,y,z)$ at a point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ are given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={x}_{0}+{f}_{x}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ y & ={y}_{0}+{f}_{y}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t, \\\\ z & ={z}_{0}+{f}_{z}({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametic equations  for the normal line to our surface that  passes through the point $(0,0,\\sqrt{e})$ can be written as \n \n\n$$\nx=0,\\,y=0,\\,z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}\n$$"] ["question-726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/id #uuid "c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b"] ["answer-84d505d2-2d10-57ad-9f74-f1753c66adec" :answer/id #uuid "84d505d2-2d10-57ad-9f74-f1753c66adec"] ["answer-84d505d2-2d10-57ad-9f74-f1753c66adec" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-84d505d2-2d10-57ad-9f74-f1753c66adec" :answer/value "x=-t,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{t\\sqrt{e}}{e}"] ["answer-f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2" :answer/id #uuid "f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2"] ["answer-f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2" :answer/value "x=0,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}"] ["answer-f43507f5-6454-586e-8980-28bd1f31c21d" :answer/id #uuid "f43507f5-6454-586e-8980-28bd1f31c21d"] ["answer-f43507f5-6454-586e-8980-28bd1f31c21d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f43507f5-6454-586e-8980-28bd1f31c21d" :answer/value "x=0,\\text{ }y=t,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+2t\\sqrt{e}"] ["answer-ad368c2c-26d6-548c-b643-eedd79a18a15" :answer/id #uuid "ad368c2c-26d6-548c-b643-eedd79a18a15"] ["answer-ad368c2c-26d6-548c-b643-eedd79a18a15" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ad368c2c-26d6-548c-b643-eedd79a18a15" :answer/value "x=t,\\text{ }y=0,\\text{ }z=\\sqrt{e}+\\frac{t\\sqrt{e}}{e}"] ["answer-7aae3ac3-02f2-5d2e-bd85-9eee8e2b917d" :answer/id #uuid "7aae3ac3-02f2-5d2e-bd85-9eee8e2b917d"] ["answer-7aae3ac3-02f2-5d2e-bd85-9eee8e2b917d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-7aae3ac3-02f2-5d2e-bd85-9eee8e2b917d" :answer/value "x=t,\\text{ }y=t,\\text{ }z=\\frac{2t\\sqrt{e}}{e}"] ["answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/correct "answer-f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2"] ["answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/choices "answer-84d505d2-2d10-57ad-9f74-f1753c66adec"] ["answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/choices "answer-f1711e24-9959-5e3d-beef-58d26c1f41b2"] ["answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/choices "answer-f43507f5-6454-586e-8980-28bd1f31c21d"] ["answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/choices "answer-ad368c2c-26d6-548c-b643-eedd79a18a15"] ["answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b" :answer-field/choices "answer-7aae3ac3-02f2-5d2e-bd85-9eee8e2b917d"] ["question-726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-c615bb4c-1597-5b7b-a91f-2e2776baf55b"] [17592186124464 :knowledge-point/questions "question-726c12fc-e2ed-50f3-8cd6-76ed12fb7e44"] ["question-056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" :question/id #uuid "056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885"] ["question-056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" :question/math-academy-id "q-131125"] ["question-056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" :question/problem "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface ${x}^{2}-{y}^{2}=4-4z$ at the point $(0,2,2).$"] ["question-056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =4-4z \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,2,2).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,2,2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}+4z)k \\\\  & =2x\\,i-2y\\,j+4\\,k \\\\ ∇f(0,2,2) & =2(0)\\,i-2(2)\\text{ }j+4\\,k \\\\  & =-4\\,j+4\\,k \\\\  & =⟨0,-4,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,2,2)$ and passes through the point $(0,2,2)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,∇f(0,2,2)=⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$"] ["question-056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/id #uuid "5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3"] ["answer-3b3d1a48-5c55-52da-b7e7-fd31db815995" :answer/id #uuid "3b3d1a48-5c55-52da-b7e7-fd31db815995"] ["answer-3b3d1a48-5c55-52da-b7e7-fd31db815995" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3b3d1a48-5c55-52da-b7e7-fd31db815995" :answer/value "⟨0,-4,-4⟩+t\\,⟨0,2,2⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["answer-af75210d-d1a7-57de-a250-0369260017a5" :answer/id #uuid "af75210d-d1a7-57de-a250-0369260017a5"] ["answer-af75210d-d1a7-57de-a250-0369260017a5" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-af75210d-d1a7-57de-a250-0369260017a5" :answer/value "⟨0,-4,-4⟩+t\\,⟨2,2,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["answer-77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05" :answer/id #uuid "77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05"] ["answer-77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05" :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨0,-4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["answer-63801628-2a57-5777-861f-90cd5a83b242" :answer/id #uuid "63801628-2a57-5777-861f-90cd5a83b242"] ["answer-63801628-2a57-5777-861f-90cd5a83b242" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-63801628-2a57-5777-861f-90cd5a83b242" :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,-4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["answer-161e6fd0-e925-55fa-8d09-6a65d107f751" :answer/id #uuid "161e6fd0-e925-55fa-8d09-6a65d107f751"] ["answer-161e6fd0-e925-55fa-8d09-6a65d107f751" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-161e6fd0-e925-55fa-8d09-6a65d107f751" :answer/value "⟨0,2,2⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,4⟩,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/correct "answer-77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05"] ["answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/choices "answer-3b3d1a48-5c55-52da-b7e7-fd31db815995"] ["answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/choices "answer-af75210d-d1a7-57de-a250-0369260017a5"] ["answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/choices "answer-77d647a0-cba4-5590-a1d5-ae95a15b5f05"] ["answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/choices "answer-63801628-2a57-5777-861f-90cd5a83b242"] ["answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3" :answer-field/choices "answer-161e6fd0-e925-55fa-8d09-6a65d107f751"] ["question-056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-5dba8cbc-5d08-5d14-930e-b1a41b746cb3"] [17592186124464 :knowledge-point/questions "question-056dc885-9ad1-5245-b1aa-85becac66885"] [17592186125735 :tutorial/content "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$ *\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental  link between\ncalculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)=  x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$  It is easy to see that  $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$ \n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$ *\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$  is parallel to the level curve.\n\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot  \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$"] [17592186125740 :tutorial/content "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if  the  gradient vector is nonzero, i.e., \n\n$$\n\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,\n$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function  \n\n$$\nw = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$ \n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt]\n&= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt]\n&= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt]\n\n\\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle.\n}\n$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)"] [17592186124731 :activity/expected-seconds 1260.0]] "retractions" [[17592186124978 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x, y) = 2x + 4y + 5xy$ that passes through the point where $(x, y) = (2, 1)$."] [17592186122468 :answer-field/choices 17592186122469] [17592186122468 :answer-field/choices 17592186122470] [17592186122468 :answer-field/choices 17592186122471] [17592186122468 :answer-field/choices 17592186122472] [17592186122468 :answer-field/choices 17592186122473] [17592186124969 :question/problem "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x, y) = 2x^{2} + 2y^{2} - y$ that passes through the point where $(x, y) = (0, 1)$."] [17592186122414 :answer-field/choices 17592186122416] [17592186122414 :answer-field/choices 17592186122419] [17592186125735 :tutorial/content "Given a function $z=f(x,y)$, if both partial derivatives $f_x,$ $f_y$ exist and are continuous in a neighborhood of a point $P(x_0,y_0)$ in the domain of the function, and if $\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0) \\neq \\mathbf 0,$ then we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(x_0,y_0)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $C$ that passes through the point $P.$*\n\nThis property is one of the most important facts in multivariable calculus since it gives a fundamental link between calculus and geometry!\n\nLet's illustrate this property with a concrete example. Consider the paraboloid $z = f (x, y)= x^2 + y^2$ and the level curve $\\color{red}C$ of $f(x,y)$ corresponding to the point $P(1,-1).$ It is easy to see that $\\color{red}C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ with equation $x^2+y^2 = 2.$\n\n![](images/f2/f2461dbfcde2403e6ed7de9d6288d33a4deb52319d35b7036628c3fa8b3789dc.png)\n\nIn this particular instance, the property says that\n\n*$\\nabla \\! f(1,-1)$ is a normal vector to the level curve $\\color{red}C$ that passes through the point $P(1,-1).$*\n\n![](images/48/481b68af522b9144e232d9c775f734cd998b31a21571cbfd983c23d0f68c3688.png)\n\nLooking at the situation in the $xy$-plane, we see the following:\n\n![](images/ce/ceb353d5164d6aa089a37e77c9469d747ef4430e363e42f9d35ee6d769d9a4e4.png)\n\nLet's verify that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is a normal line to the level curve $C$ at the point $P(1,-1).$\n\n- First, computing the gradient, we find that $\\nabla f = \\langle 2x, 2y \\rangle.$ Therefore, $$\\nabla f(1,-1) = \\langle 2, -2 \\rangle.$$\n- Then, using elementary calculus, we can show that the equation of the tangent to $C$ at $P$ is $y=x-2.$ Since this line has slope $m=1,$ it follows that the vector $\\langle 1,1 \\rangle$ is parallel to the level curve.\n- Finally, taking the dot product between the gradient and the tangent vector, we obtain $$\\nabla f(1,-1)\\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = \\langle 2, -2\\rangle \\cdot \\langle 1,1 \\rangle = 0.$$\n\nThis means that $\\nabla f(1,-1)$ is *perpendicular* to the tangent line to $C$ at $(1,-1).$"] [17592186125740 :tutorial/content "The same property holds true in the case of three or more variables. In general, if $f$ is a differentiable real-valued function of $n$ variables, $\\mathbf y$ is a point in the domain of $f,$ and if the gradient vector is nonzero, i.e.,\n\n$$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)= \\left \\langle f_{x_1}(\\mathbf y), f_{x_2}(\\mathbf y), \\cdots, f_{x_n}(\\mathbf y)\\right \\rangle \\neq \\mathbf 0,$$\n\nthen we have the following result:\n\n*$\\nabla f (\\mathbf y)$ is a normal vector to the level surface $f(x_1,x_2, \\cdots, x_n) =f(\\mathbf y)$ at $\\mathbf y.$*\n\nTo illustrate, let's consider the function\n\n$$w = f (x, y, z)= \\dfrac 1 2\\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2-1\\right).$$\n\nThe level surface corresponding to $w=0$ is the sphere $\\mathcal S$ with equation $x^2 + y^2 +z^2 =1.$\n\nThe point $P(0,0,1)$ lies on $\\mathcal S.$ Computing $\\nabla f(0,0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\\eqalign{ \\nabla f(x,y,z) &= \\langle f_x, \\: f_y, \\:f_z\\rangle \\[5pt] &= \\dfrac12\\langle2x, \\: 2y, \\: 2z\\rangle\\[5pt] &= \\langle x, \\: y, \\: z\\rangle\\[10pt] \\nabla f(0,0,1) &= \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1 \\rangle. }$$\n\nNow clearly, $\\nabla f (0,0, 1)$ is perpendicular to $\\mathcal S$ at the point $(0,0,1).$\n\n![](images/88/88bf25de31ae3318379865cdf2590c15216cb984cbf3948f975caa47317be664.png)"]]}]